  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying the outcome with the minimum probability in each lottery and comparing their favorability. While the individual does consider the highest outcomes (which coincidentally are also the least probable in both lotteries), they do not explicitly identify or reason about these outcomes as the 'least probable' ones. Instead, their justification centers on the higher payoff and similar probabilities, suggesting a heuristic like 'go for the bigger prize when probabilities are close' or an intuitive expected value calculation. There is no indication that the structural rule of the decision reason — comparing the most favorable outcomes specifically because they have the minimum probability — was applied. The reasoning aligns loosely in outcome (choosing B) but not in process. Therefore, while the relevant information (high outcomes and their probabilities) is partially considered, the rule of the decision reason is not reflected in the justification.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
